WEBVTT

NOTE Caption timing is approximate. Synthetic narration: Gemini Kore.

00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:04.265
Where would you steer? The opening is on

00:00:04.265 --> 00:00:07.279
the left. For us, that's easy. For this

00:00:07.279 --> 00:00:10.294
little car, it means sending a picture, asking

00:00:10.294 --> 00:00:13.309
a question, and waiting for an answer. Let's

00:00:13.309 --> 00:00:15.570
follow that journey in Pocket Pilot.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.373
First, capture the road. Ask: which lane is

00:00:20.373 --> 00:00:23.846
open? Give the model three allowed answers: left,

00:00:23.846 --> 00:00:27.320
middle, or right. It returns a choice. Our

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.793
code checks that answer, then moves the car.

00:00:30.793 --> 00:00:34.266
The model suggests; the application controls what happens

00:00:34.266 --> 00:00:34.700
next.

00:00:36.025 --> 00:00:38.869
A language model works with text. A vision

00:00:38.869 --> 00:00:41.714
language model can also read images. An API

00:00:41.714 --> 00:00:44.558
is the interface our code uses to ask

00:00:44.558 --> 00:00:47.403
either one a question. Jev is a model

00:00:47.403 --> 00:00:50.247
from TypeSafe, using text and JSON here. It

00:00:50.247 --> 00:00:52.025
isn't the Java programming language.

00:00:53.358 --> 00:00:56.238
With a Decisions API, we define the question

00:00:56.238 --> 00:00:59.118
and the shape of its answer before making

00:00:59.118 --> 00:01:01.998
the call. Here is a shortened example: a

00:01:01.998 --> 00:01:04.878
road image goes in, and a lane choice

00:01:04.878 --> 00:01:07.758
comes back. Only that lane answer is needed

00:01:07.758 --> 00:01:08.478
to steer.

00:01:09.817 --> 00:01:13.116
Choice picks a label, with probabilities and a

00:01:13.116 --> 00:01:16.416
separate confidence value. Noul answers a yes-or-no question

00:01:16.416 --> 00:01:19.715
with a number between zero and one. Score

00:01:19.715 --> 00:01:23.015
places an answer on ordered levels, so fractions

00:01:23.015 --> 00:01:26.314
are possible. These are estimates. Even a confidence

00:01:26.314 --> 00:01:28.377
of one can be wrong.

00:01:29.692 --> 00:01:32.681
A conventional model can explain the road. It

00:01:32.681 --> 00:01:35.670
can also return JSON that follows a schema.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:38.659
Decisions is useful when we already know the

00:01:38.659 --> 00:01:41.648
possible answers and want typed results. Whether a

00:01:41.648 --> 00:01:44.637
particular model is faster or better is something

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:46.132
to measure, not assume.

00:01:47.442 --> 00:01:50.266
Watch what happens while an answer is on

00:01:50.266 --> 00:01:53.090
its way. If the road keeps moving, that

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:55.915
answer can arrive too late. Classroom mode pauses

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:58.739
the game clock until a fresh decision returns.

00:01:58.739 --> 00:02:01.563
Real time mode exposes that delay. A correct

00:02:01.563 --> 00:02:03.682
answer can still miss its deadline.

00:02:04.983 --> 00:02:07.838
Here are all six models in the same

00:02:07.838 --> 00:02:10.692
one-question comparison. For images, Luna took about six

00:02:10.692 --> 00:02:13.547
tenths of a second; the chat models, about

00:02:13.547 --> 00:02:16.401
one and a half seconds. With structured JSON,

00:02:16.401 --> 00:02:19.255
Jev and Luna were both near half a

00:02:19.255 --> 00:02:22.110
second. Each number is a median of just

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:22.823
three scenes.

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:27.563
The full run used sixty-three settings and a

00:02:27.563 --> 00:02:30.977
hundred and eighty-nine attempts. We saw a hundred

00:02:30.977 --> 00:02:34.390
and sixty-eight correct lane choices, three wrong choices,

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:37.803
and eighteen request-size errors. These timings include the

00:02:37.803 --> 00:02:41.217
network and the complete response. This small experiment

00:02:41.217 --> 00:02:44.630
is a starting point, not a universal ranking.

00:02:45.942 --> 00:02:49.949
For the Sustainability Lab, start with a photo-quality

00:02:49.949 --> 00:02:53.956
check. Is this image usable, blurred, or obstructed?

00:02:53.956 --> 00:02:57.963
We can label examples ourselves and check the

00:02:57.963 --> 00:03:01.971
model's mistakes. Later, try smoke or solar-panel review.

00:03:01.971 --> 00:03:05.978
Those are proposed applications; a smoke photograph alone

00:03:05.978 --> 00:03:07.982
cannot measure particulate concentration.

00:03:09.317 --> 00:03:12.776
Another idea: a sensor reading stays at zero,

00:03:12.776 --> 00:03:16.235
while a field note mentions a power interruption.

00:03:16.235 --> 00:03:19.694
Combine the readings and context, then suggest what

00:03:19.694 --> 00:03:23.153
a researcher should check. Compare with simple rules.

00:03:23.153 --> 00:03:26.612
Keep the original data, and send uncertain cases

00:03:26.612 --> 00:03:27.477
for review.

00:03:28.817 --> 00:03:31.871
Open the game and choose a model, an

00:03:31.871 --> 00:03:34.924
input, and a question count. Start in classroom

00:03:34.924 --> 00:03:37.978
mode. Follow one decision through its image, question,

00:03:37.978 --> 00:03:41.032
answer, and timing. Pause, go back in the

00:03:41.032 --> 00:03:44.086
log, and open the JSON. That's where this

00:03:44.086 --> 00:03:46.377
small game becomes a useful experiment.
